export const H1={title:'The home health aide: role, responsibilities and law',hook:'A home health aide works where clients live: their kitchens, bedrooms and bathrooms. That trust brings responsibility. Aides follow a care plan written by a nurse, observe and report changes, protect clients’ rights and know exactly which tasks they may and may not perform.',objectives:['Describe the Home Health Aide event, test plan and Round Two skills.','Explain the home health team, the care plan and the aide’s scope of practice.','Describe client rights and legal and ethical responsibilities.','Recognize and report abuse, neglect and exploitation.'],sections:[
['The Home Health Aide event',[
'Round One is a **50-question multiple-choice test in 60 minutes**, with no time announcements at ILC. The test plan: **responsibilities and legal/ethical issues 10%**, **effective communication 6%**, **infection control and safety 10%**, **body systems and common disorders 40%**, **caring for special populations 24%** and **basic healthcare skills 10%**. Questions come from Balduzzi’s *Homemaker Home Health Aide* and Leahy, Fuzy and Grafe’s *Providing Home Care*. Top scorers advance to Round Two, a written-scenario skills assessment: **Skill I** adult tympanic temperature (3 minutes), **II** apical pulse (3), **III** emptying a urinary drainage unit (4), **IV** changing a dry dressing using non-sterile technique (5), **V** measuring and recording intake and output (5), **VI** caring for dentures (5), **VII** moving a patient up in bed using a drawsheet (3) and **VIII** applying elasticized stockings to one leg (5).',
'Body fluids are simulated, scenarios may combine skills, steps must be performed or verbalized if equipment is missing, and judges stop any unsafe step. Competitors bring photo ID, two #2 pencils, a **manual watch with a second hand** (no smartwatches), ½-inch or 1-inch tape, bandage scissors and **three pairs of non-latex gloves**. To be recognized as an award winner, competitors need **70% or higher on the combined Round Two skills**; final rank adds test and skill scores, with ties going to the higher test score.',
match('Match the skill to its time limit',[['Tympanic temperature','3 minutes'],['Apical pulse','3 minutes'],['Emptying a urinary drainage unit','4 minutes'],['Dry dressing change','5 minutes'],['Intake and output','5 minutes'],['Moving up in bed with a drawsheet','3 minutes']])]],
['The home health team and the care plan',[
'Home health care is ordered by a **physician or other authorized practitioner** and managed by a **home health agency**. A **registered nurse (case manager or supervisor)** assesses the client and writes the **care plan**, which lists the tasks the aide performs, how often and what to report. Other team members include **physical, occupational and speech therapists**, **medical social workers**, **dietitians** and the client and family, who are central members. Medicare covers home health for clients who are **homebound** and need intermittent skilled care under a plan of care. Federal rules for Medicare-certified agencies require aides to complete training of **at least 75 hours, including at least 16 hours of supervised practical training**, pass a competency evaluation, and receive regular supervision by a registered nurse.',
'**Homemaker** tasks include light housekeeping, laundry, shopping and meal preparation; **personal care** includes bathing, grooming, dressing, toileting, mobility and feeding. Aides follow the care plan exactly; if a client or family asks for something outside it—such as changing medication doses or performing a sterile procedure—the aide politely declines and contacts the supervisor.',
sort('Within a home health aide’s role?',['Usually within the role','Outside the role'],[['Assisting with a bath',0],['Preparing a low-sodium meal from the care plan',0],['Reminding a client to take medication',0],['Changing a client’s insulin dose',1],['Performing sterile catheter insertion',1],['Taking and recording vital signs',0],['Diagnosing a rash',1],['Light housekeeping in the client’s areas',0]])]],
['Client rights',[
'Clients receiving home care have rights, including the right to be treated with **dignity and respect**, to **privacy and confidentiality** (protected under **HIPAA**), to **participate in care planning**, to **refuse care**, to **voice complaints** without retaliation, to have their **property respected** and to be **free from abuse, neglect and restraint**. Aides knock and identify themselves, ask permission before entering rooms or handling belongings, explain each task, offer choices and keep information private. Aides never accept money or gifts, borrow or lend, or discuss their own personal problems—**professional boundaries** protect both client and aide.',
chk('A client refuses a bath today. What should the aide do?',['Respect the refusal, offer an alternative time or option and report it to the supervisor','Insist the bath happen now','Give the bath anyway because it is on the care plan','Ignore it without documenting'],'Clients have the right to refuse; report refusals.')]],
['Legal and ethical responsibilities',[
'**Ethics** are principles of right and wrong; **laws** are rules enforced by government. Legal terms aides must know include **negligence** (failing to give the care a reasonable aide would, causing harm), **malpractice** (negligence by a professional), **assault** (threatening to harm), **battery** (touching without permission), **false imprisonment** (restraining or keeping someone without consent), **invasion of privacy** and **defamation** (false statements that damage reputation—**slander** spoken, **libel** written). Aides protect themselves and clients by following the care plan, staying within their scope, documenting accurately and reporting changes. **Documentation** is a legal record: write in ink, record facts and times, never chart care before it is done and correct errors with a single line, the date and initials.',
match('Match the legal term to the example',[['Battery','Bathing a client who has refused'],['False imprisonment','Locking a client in a room'],['Negligence','Leaving bed rails down for a client at high fall risk, leading to a fall'],['Invasion of privacy','Discussing a client’s diagnosis with neighbors'],['Assault','Threatening to withhold dinner'],['Libel','Writing false, damaging statements about a client']])]],
['Abuse, neglect and exploitation',[
'Types of abuse include **physical**, **emotional (psychological)**, **sexual** and **financial exploitation**; **neglect** is failure to provide needed care, and **self-neglect** occurs when clients cannot meet their own needs. Signs include unexplained bruises or burns, fear of a caregiver, poor hygiene, weight loss, missing money or belongings, untreated medical problems and a caregiver who will not let the client speak alone. Home health aides are often the only regular visitors, so they must **report suspected abuse** to their supervisor immediately, following agency policy and state law; adult protective services or child protective services investigate. Aides document objectively—“bruise 3 inches wide on left upper arm; client states, ‘I fell’”—without accusing anyone.',
kase('A worrying visit',{a:{text:'During a visit, you notice finger-shaped bruises on a client’s upper arm. Her son answers every question for her and stands nearby. What should you do?',choices:[['Report your observations to your supervisor right away and document them objectively','b','Correct. Suspected abuse must be reported immediately.',true],['Confront the son about the bruises','b','Confrontation may endanger the client; report through proper channels.',false]]},b:{text:'The client later whispers, “Please don’t tell anyone.” How do you respond?',choices:[['Explain kindly that you must report concerns to keep her safe','c','Correct. Aides cannot promise secrecy about abuse.',true],['Promise to keep it a secret','c','Reporting is a legal and ethical duty.',false]]},c:{text:'Your supervisor contacts adult protective services, and you continue to document each visit carefully.',end:true}})]],
["Working in clients’ homes",["Home care requires independence and planning. Aides often visit several clients a day, so they plan routes, arrive on time, call the agency if delayed and organize each visit around the care plan—starting with priorities such as medication reminders, toileting and meals, then personal care and homemaking. Each home is the client’s private space: knock and wait to be invited in, ask before opening cupboards or moving belongings, follow household routines where safe and use only the client’s supplies designated for care. Respect cultural and religious practices about food, modesty, touch and decision-making.","**Personal safety** matters too. Aides know the address and directions, park in well-lit areas, keep their phone charged, lock their car and keep valuables out of sight. If a visit feels unsafe—an aggressive person, weapons, illegal activity or an unrestrained dangerous animal—they **leave and call the agency** (or 911 in an emergency) rather than staying. Agencies have policies on cell phone use, transportation of clients, accepting gifts and documenting visit times; following them protects both aide and client.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Professional in a client’s home?","buckets":["Professional","Not professional"],"items":[["Knocking and waiting to be invited in",0],["Calling the agency when running late",0],["Using the client’s phone for personal calls",1],["Leaving and reporting when a visit is unsafe",0],["Accepting cash as a thank-you gift",1],["Asking before moving the client’s belongings",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Test: 50 questions in 60 minutes, with body systems 40% and special populations 24%; eight Round Two skills need 70% combined to be recognized.','Aides follow the RN’s care plan, stay within scope (no medication changes, diagnosis or sterile procedures) and report changes.','Respect client rights and boundaries, document facts in ink and report suspected abuse, neglect or exploitation immediately.'],terms:[['Care plan','Nurse-written plan listing tasks and observations for each client.'],['Homebound','Leaving home requires considerable effort; a Medicare home health criterion.'],['Neglect','Failure to provide needed care or services.']],mcqs:[q('Which test area has the largest weight?','Body systems and common disorders (40%)','Effective communication (6%)','Infection control (10%)','Basic skills (10%)','Special populations is 24%.'),q('Who writes the home health aide’s care plan?','A registered nurse','The aide','A family friend','The client’s neighbor','The aide follows it.'),q('Touching a client without permission is:','Battery','Libel','Slander','Assault only','Assault is the threat.'),q('Which task is outside an aide’s role?','Changing a medication dose','Light housekeeping','Assisting with dressing','Taking vital signs','Report medication questions.'),q('Federal rules require Medicare-certified agency aides to complete at least:','75 hours of training including 16 supervised practical hours','10 hours online','A bachelor’s degree','500 hours of training','Plus a competency evaluation.'),q('What should the aide do if they suspect abuse?','Report to the supervisor immediately','Wait until the next visit','Confront the suspected abuser','Do nothing without proof','Reporting is mandatory.'),q('How is a charting error corrected?','Single line, date and initials','Correction fluid','Erasing','Tearing out the page','Records are legal documents.'),q('What equipment must competitors bring for Round Two?','A manual watch with a second hand, tape, bandage scissors and gloves','A smartwatch','A blood pressure machine','A thermometer','Smartwatches are not allowed.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the home health aide’s role on the home health team, including the care plan, scope of practice, client rights and legal responsibilities.',rubric:[['Team and care plan',['nurse','care plan','agency','therapist','social worker']],['Scope of practice',['scope','medication','sterile','report','supervisor']],['Client rights',['dignity','privacy','refuse','hipaa','boundar']],['Legal responsibilities',['negligence','battery','document','abuse','report']]],model:'The home health aide works for a home health agency under the supervision of a registered nurse, who assesses the client and writes the care plan that lists the aide’s tasks, frequency and what to report; the team also includes the physician, therapists, social workers, dietitians and the client and family. Aides provide personal care, homemaking and observations but stay within their scope: they do not change medications, diagnose, perform sterile procedures or take orders outside the care plan—they report requests and changes to the supervisor. They protect client rights to dignity, privacy under HIPAA, participation in care, refusing care and freedom from abuse, and they keep professional boundaries by refusing gifts and money. Legally, aides avoid negligence by following the care plan, avoid battery by obtaining permission before touching, avoid false imprisonment and invasion of privacy, document facts accurately in ink and report suspected abuse, neglect or exploitation immediately.'},sources:[RULES,MEDICARE_HH,ECFR_HHA,ACL_ABUSE]};

export const H2={title:'Communication, observation and reporting',hook:'The home health aide is the team’s eyes and ears. A nurse might visit once a week, but the aide sees the client several times—and notices the new cough, the untouched lunch or the confusion that was not there yesterday. Clear communication and accurate reporting turn those observations into timely care.',objectives:['Apply verbal and nonverbal communication skills with clients and families.','Adapt communication for hearing loss, vision loss, aphasia and confusion.','Distinguish objective and subjective observations and report changes promptly.','Complete the daily record sheet accurately.'],sections:[
['Communication basics',[
'Communication is a two-way process of sending and receiving messages. **Verbal** communication uses words; **nonverbal** communication includes facial expressions, tone of voice, posture, gestures and touch. Effective aides introduce themselves, use the client’s preferred name, speak clearly and calmly, face the client, use simple words and short sentences and check understanding. **Active listening**—giving full attention, nodding, reflecting feelings and asking open-ended questions—helps clients feel heard. **Barriers** include giving advice, false reassurance (“Don’t worry, everything will be fine”), changing the subject, using medical jargon, interrupting, asking “why” questions that sound judgmental and arguing. Cultural differences in language, eye contact, personal space and family roles should be respected.',
sort('Helpful or a barrier?',['Helpful','Barrier'],[['“Tell me more about how you’re feeling.”',0],['“Don’t worry, you’ll be fine.”',1],['Sitting at eye level while talking',0],['Using medical abbreviations with the client',1],['Pausing to let the client finish',0],['“Why didn’t you take your pills?”',1]])]],
['Adapting communication',[
'For clients with **hearing loss**, reduce background noise, face them in good light, speak slightly louder and lower in pitch without shouting, use gestures or writing and make sure **hearing aids** are clean, inserted and working. For **vision loss**, announce yourself when entering, explain what you are doing, keep furniture in the same place, describe food positions using the **clock method** (“potatoes at 3 o’clock”) and offer your arm when walking. For **aphasia** after a stroke, ask yes-or-no questions, give time to respond, use picture boards and do not finish sentences unless invited. For clients with **dementia**, approach from the front, use one-step directions, a calm tone and their name, and avoid arguing; redirect instead. For clients who speak another language, use agency-approved interpreters rather than family members whenever possible.',
match('Match the need to the adaptation',[['Hearing loss','Face the client and check the hearing aid'],['Vision loss','Use the clock method to describe meals'],['Expressive aphasia','Ask yes-or-no questions and allow time'],['Dementia','Use one-step directions and redirect'],['Language barrier','Use an agency-approved interpreter'],['Anxious client','Speak calmly and explain each step']])]],
['Observing and reporting',[
'**Objective** observations are facts you can see, hear, smell or measure: “Client’s temperature is 100.8°F,” “ate 25% of lunch,” “urine is dark amber with a strong odor.” **Subjective** observations are what the client tells you that you cannot verify: “I feel dizzy,” “my chest hurts.” Record subjective statements in the client’s own words in quotation marks. Use your senses—sight (skin color, swelling), hearing (breathing sounds, complaints), smell (odors of urine, infection, alcohol) and touch (skin temperature, moisture). Some changes must be reported **immediately** to the supervisor: chest pain, trouble breathing, signs of stroke, severe bleeding, a fall, sudden confusion, a temperature outside the range in the care plan, no urine for **8 hours**, a refusal of care or signs of abuse. In an emergency, call **911** first, then the agency.',
sort('Objective or subjective?',['Objective','Subjective'],[['Pulse 112 beats per minute',0],['“My stomach hurts.”',1],['Reddened area on the right heel',0],['“I feel tired today.”',1],['Ate half of breakfast',0],['“I’m worried about my son.”',1]])]],
['The daily record and documentation',[
'In Round Two, competitors record results on the **HOSA Home Health Services Daily Record**, which lists the client, day and date and rows for **bath, grooming, dressing, mobility, catheter, temperature, pulse, respiration and blood pressure**, with columns for **treatment/results** and **observations/patient reaction**, space for other instructions and additional observations, and the aide’s **signature and date**. Many rating sheets award **4 points**—the largest single step—for accurate recording. Good documentation is **accurate, complete, objective, timely and legible**; use the agency’s approved abbreviations, record the time (many agencies use the **24-hour clock**, where 2:30 p.m. is 1430), never document before performing care and sign every entry.',
{t:'example',title:'Converting to the 24-hour clock',prompt:'Record these times in 24-hour format: 7:15 a.m., 12:00 noon, 4:45 p.m. and 11:30 p.m.',steps:['Morning times stay the same, written with four digits: 7:15 a.m. → 0715.','Noon is 1200.','For p.m. times after noon, add 12 to the hour: 4:45 p.m. → 1645.','11:30 p.m. → 2330.'],answer:'0715, 1200, 1645 and 2330.'},
chk('Which entry is best for the daily record?',['“1030: Ate 50% of breakfast. States, ‘I’m not hungry.’ Reported to supervisor.”','“Client was being difficult.”','“Seemed fine I think.”','“Will take temp later”—written before taking it'],'Objective, timed entries with the client’s words are best.')]],
['Working with families and the team',[
'Families are part of the care team, and the home is their space. Aides respect family routines and decisions, explain what they are doing, teach simple safety tips when the care plan allows and refer questions about diagnoses or medications to the nurse. Conflicts—such as a family member who wants different care—are handled calmly and reported to the supervisor rather than argued. Aides communicate with the supervisor by phone or the agency’s system, give clear, organized reports and ask when unsure. Professional communication also includes punctuality, calling ahead if delayed and protecting confidentiality on phones and in conversations.',
kase('A family request',{a:{text:'A client’s daughter asks you to give her mother an extra pain pill because “she seems uncomfortable.” What should you do?',choices:[['Explain that you cannot change medications and call the supervisor about the pain','b','Correct. Medication decisions are outside the aide’s role; pain should be reported.',true],['Give the extra pill to help','b','Giving unauthorized medication is outside scope and unsafe.',false]]},b:{text:'How do you document the event?',choices:[['Record the client’s pain report, the daughter’s request and that you notified the supervisor, with times','c','Correct. Objective, timed documentation protects everyone.',true],['Do not document family requests','c','Requests and actions should be recorded.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse calls the physician, who adjusts the pain plan, and the family thanks you for handling it safely.',end:true}})]],
["Reporting to the supervisor",["A good report is brief, organized and factual. Many agencies use a structure similar to **SBAR**: **Situation** (what is happening now), **Background** (relevant history), **Assessment** (what you observed and measured—aides report observations, not diagnoses) and **Request** (what you need, such as a nurse visit or instructions). Before calling, gather the client’s name, the time, vital signs and the specific changes you noticed. Speak clearly, repeat back any instructions you receive and document the call, the person you spoke with and the instructions.",{"t":"example","title":"A model phone report","prompt":"Your client, Mr. Lopez, has a temperature of 100.9°F tympanic, is coughing more and ate only a few bites of lunch. Write a short report to your supervisor.","steps":["Situation: identify yourself, the client and the concern.","Background: note relevant history from the care plan.","Observations: give measured values and what you saw and heard.","Request: ask what to do next and repeat back the instructions."],"answer":"“This is Dana, the aide for Mr. Lopez. At 1300 his temperature is 100.9°F tympanic and he is coughing more than yesterday. He has COPD. He ate about 10% of lunch and says, ‘I feel weak.’ His pulse is 104 and respirations 24. Can the nurse see him today? … I will encourage fluids and call back if his breathing gets worse.”"},{"t":"check","q":"What should an aide do after receiving instructions by phone?","options":["Repeat them back and document the call and instructions","Rely on memory only","Ask the family to remember them","Wait to document until the next week"],"why":"Read-back prevents errors."}]]
],takeaways:['Use clear verbal and nonverbal communication and active listening; avoid false reassurance, jargon and judgmental “why” questions.','Adapt for hearing and vision loss, aphasia, dementia and language barriers (clock method, one-step directions, interpreters).','Record objective facts and the client’s own words; report urgent changes immediately (no urine for 8 hours, falls, chest pain, confusion).'],terms:[['Objective observation','A fact that can be seen, measured or verified.'],['Subjective observation','Information the client reports that cannot be measured.'],['Active listening','Fully attending to and reflecting a speaker’s message.']],mcqs:[q('Which is an objective observation?','Temperature 100.8°F','“I feel hot.”','“My head hurts.”','“I’m worried.”','Objective data can be measured.'),q('For a client with vision loss, food can be described using:','The clock method','Medical abbreviations','Color only','No description','“Peas at 9 o’clock.”'),q('Which response is a communication barrier?','“Don’t worry, everything will be fine.”','“Tell me more.”','“You seem upset.”','Nodding while listening','False reassurance blocks communication.'),q('How should a client’s complaint be documented?','In the client’s own words in quotation marks','As the aide’s opinion','It should not be documented','With a diagnosis','Record subjective statements exactly.'),q('In 24-hour time, 3:20 p.m. is:','1520','0320','1320','2320','Add 12 to p.m. hours.'),q('Which change should be reported immediately?','The client has not urinated in 8 hours','The client watched television','The client ate all of lunch','The client slept well','The HOSA I&O sheet notes this rule.'),q('For a client with aphasia, the aide should:','Ask yes-or-no questions and allow extra time','Speak very quickly','Finish all their sentences','Avoid talking to them','Patience supports communication.'),q('Which step on most HHA rating sheets is worth the most points?','Accurately recording on the daily record sheet (4 points)','Gathering equipment (1 point)','Turning on the thermometer (1 point)','Placing the probe cover (1 point)','Documentation is heavily weighted.')],frq:{prompt:'A client recovering from a stroke has expressive aphasia and mild hearing loss. Describe how you would communicate with them, what observations you would make and report, and how you would document a visit.',rubric:[['Adapted communication',['yes-or-no','time','picture','face','hearing aid','noise']],['Observations',['objective','subjective','vital','skin','intake','mood','pain']],['Reporting',['report','supervisor','immediately','change','911']],['Documentation',['daily record','time','own words','ink','sign','accurate']]],model:'I would reduce background noise, face the client in good light, make sure the hearing aid is in and working and speak clearly in a slightly lower pitch without shouting. Because of expressive aphasia, I would ask yes-or-no questions, give extra time to respond, use a picture or word board and avoid finishing sentences unless invited, watching facial expressions and gestures. I would observe objective signs such as vital signs, skin color and condition, how much they eat and drink, mobility and urine output, and note subjective reports like pain in their own words. I would report immediately any new weakness, facial drooping, worsening speech, chest pain, falls, sudden confusion or no urine for eight hours, calling 911 first in an emergency. On the daily record I would document the time, care given, measurements and observations objectively in ink, include the client’s statements in quotation marks and sign and date the entry.'},sources:[RULES,HIPAA,MEDICARE_HH]};

export const H3={title:'Infection control and safety in the home',hook:'In a hospital, sinks, gloves and sharps containers are everywhere. In a client’s home, the aide brings infection control with them—and also has to notice the loose rug, the overloaded outlet and the oxygen tank next to a candle. Infection control and safety make up 10% of the test and appear in every skill as “standard precautions.”',objectives:['Apply the chain of infection and standard precautions in home care.','Perform hand hygiene and use gloves and other PPE correctly.','Handle laundry, waste, sharps and spills safely at home.','Prevent falls, fires, oxygen hazards and injuries using good body mechanics.'],sections:[
['The chain of infection and standard precautions',[
'Infection spreads through a **chain of infection**: an **infectious agent**, a **reservoir**, a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission** (contact, droplet, airborne, vehicles such as food or water, and vectors), a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Breaking any link prevents infection, and **hand hygiene** breaks the most important one. **Standard precautions** are used with **every client**: treat blood, body fluids (except sweat), non-intact skin and mucous membranes as potentially infectious; perform hand hygiene; wear gloves and other PPE when contact is expected; handle sharps and contaminated items safely; and clean and disinfect equipment. **Transmission-based precautions** (contact, droplet, airborne) are added for known or suspected infections according to the care plan.',
order('Arrange the chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Hand hygiene and gloves',[
'Wash with **soap and water** when hands are visibly soiled, before eating and after using the restroom, and when caring for clients with *C. difficile* diarrhea; otherwise **alcohol-based hand rub** is effective. Handwashing technique: wet hands, apply soap, lather all surfaces including between fingers and under nails for **at least 20 seconds**, rinse with fingertips pointing down, dry with a clean paper towel and use the towel to turn off the faucet. Perform hand hygiene **before and after client contact**, **before putting on and after removing gloves**, after contact with body fluids or contaminated items and before preparing food. Wear gloves for contact with body fluids, mucous membranes or non-intact skin; change them between tasks and remove them by peeling them inside out without touching the outside with bare hands. Every HOSA HHA skill awards points for hand hygiene and for “practiced standard precautions throughout.”',
chk('When must hands be washed with soap and water rather than hand rub?',['When hands are visibly soiled','Before every conversation','Only at the end of the day','Never'],'Alcohol rub does not remove visible soil.')]],
['Infection control tasks in the home',[
'Aides often carry supplies in a **bag** and use **bag technique**: place the bag on a clean, dry surface (on a paper barrier if needed), wash hands before reaching in and keep clean and dirty items separate. Clean the **bathroom and kitchen** regularly and disinfect surfaces touched by body fluids with an appropriate disinfectant. Handle **soiled laundry** with gloves, hold it away from the body, do not shake it and wash it separately in hot water with detergent (bleach as allowed). Place contaminated waste such as dressings in a sealed plastic bag before putting it in the household trash, unless agency rules differ. **Sharps** such as lancets and needles go in a **puncture-resistant container**—a sharps container or, if allowed locally, a heavy plastic bottle with a screw cap—labeled and disposed of according to local rules. Follow **food safety**: wash hands and surfaces, separate raw and cooked foods, cook to safe temperatures and refrigerate promptly.',
sort('Correct infection-control practice?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Holding soiled linens away from your uniform',0],['Shaking dirty linens to remove crumbs',1],['Placing lancets in a puncture-resistant container',0],['Throwing used needles directly into the wastebasket',1],['Bagging soiled dressings before discarding',0],['Using the same gloves for toileting and food preparation',1]])]],
['Home safety and fall prevention',[
'Falls are a leading cause of injury among older adults. During each visit, aides look for hazards: **loose rugs**, clutter and cords in walkways, poor lighting, slippery bathtubs without grab bars or nonslip mats, unstable furniture and pets underfoot. Prevention includes clear pathways, night-lights, **nonskid footwear**, grab bars and shower chairs, keeping frequently used items within reach, using assistive devices correctly and encouraging clients to rise slowly to prevent dizziness. If a client starts to fall, do not try to stop it: widen your stance, bring the client close and **ease them to the floor**, protecting the head. Do not lift them afterward; check for injury, call for help per agency policy and report every fall.',
kase('Assisting a fall',{a:{text:'While walking with you, a client’s knees buckle. What should you do?',choices:[['Stand with feet apart, pull the client close and slide them gently to the floor','b','Correct. Easing to the floor prevents injury to both of you.',true],['Try to hold the client upright','b','Fighting a fall can injure you and the client.',false]]},b:{text:'The client is on the floor and says their hip hurts. What next?',choices:[['Keep them still and comfortable, call for help or 911 and notify the supervisor','c','Correct. Do not move a possibly injured client.',true],['Lift them back to bed by yourself','c','Lifting alone could worsen an injury.',false]]},c:{text:'Emergency services arrive, and you document what happened and report the fall to your agency.',end:true}})]],
['Fire, oxygen and body mechanics',[
'**Fire safety** includes working smoke detectors, safe use of space heaters, not overloading outlets, never leaving cooking unattended and having an escape plan. If fire occurs, follow **RACE**—**R**escue, **A**larm, **C**ontain, **E**xtinguish or evacuate—and use an extinguisher with **PASS**—**P**ull, **A**im at the base, **S**queeze, **S**weep. **Oxygen** supports combustion: post “oxygen in use” signs, allow **no smoking, candles or open flames**, keep tanks secured upright and away from heat sources, avoid petroleum-based products on the face and check that tubing is not a tripping hazard. **Body mechanics** protect the aide: keep a **wide base of support** with feet about shoulder-width apart, bend at the knees and hips rather than the waist, keep the back straight, hold objects close to the body, avoid twisting (pivot the feet instead), push rather than pull and get help for heavy lifts.',
flip('Safety acronyms and rules',[['RACE','Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish/evacuate'],['PASS','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep'],['Oxygen in use','No smoking, candles or open flames'],['Base of support','Feet about shoulder-width apart'],['Lifting','Bend knees, keep back straight, hold load close'],['Turning','Pivot the feet instead of twisting the back']])]],
["Transmission-based precautions and staying healthy",["When a client has a known or suspected infection, the nurse adds **transmission-based precautions** to the care plan. **Contact precautions** (for example, MRSA or *C. difficile*) add gloves and a gown for contact with the client or their environment; for *C. difficile*, wash hands with **soap and water** because alcohol rub does not kill its spores, and clean with a bleach-based disinfectant as directed. **Droplet precautions** (for example, influenza) add a **mask** when close to the client. **Airborne precautions** (for example, tuberculosis) require a fitted respirator such as an **N95** for staff who are trained and fit-tested; in homes, these clients are usually cared for under specific agency plans. Dedicate equipment such as thermometers to one client when possible.","Aides protect themselves and clients by staying current with recommended **vaccines**—including influenza and hepatitis B, which employers must offer to workers with exposure risk—covering coughs and sneezes, and **staying home when sick** with fever, vomiting or diarrhea, notifying the agency. Clients with weak immune systems, such as those receiving chemotherapy, are especially vulnerable.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the infection to the added precaution","pairs":[["MRSA wound infection","Contact: gloves and gown"],["C. difficile diarrhea","Contact plus soap-and-water handwashing"],["Influenza","Droplet: mask within close range"],["Tuberculosis","Airborne: fit-tested respirator"],["Any client","Standard precautions"],["Aide with fever and vomiting","Stay home and notify the agency"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Break the chain of infection; standard precautions for every client; hand hygiene before and after contact and glove use (soap and water for visible soil, 20 seconds).','Bag soiled waste, handle laundry away from the body, place sharps in puncture-resistant containers and follow food safety.','Remove fall hazards and ease a falling client to the floor; RACE and PASS for fire; no flames near oxygen; use good body mechanics.'],terms:[['Standard precautions','Infection-control practices used with every client.'],['Bag technique','Keeping the supply bag clean and separate from contamination.'],['Body mechanics','Safe, efficient use of the body during movement.']],mcqs:[q('Which practice breaks the chain of infection most effectively?','Hand hygiene','Wearing jewelry','Sharing towels','Shaking linens','It interrupts transmission.'),q('Hands should be lathered for at least:','20 seconds','2 seconds','5 seconds','2 minutes','Include between fingers and under nails.'),q('Used lancets at home should go in:','A puncture-resistant container','A paper bag','The toilet','The kitchen trash loose','Follow local disposal rules.'),q('If a client begins to fall, the aide should:','Ease the client to the floor while protecting the head','Hold them upright at all costs','Let go and step back','Lift them quickly','Do not fight the fall.'),q('In PASS, the extinguisher is aimed at:','The base of the fire','The top of the flames','The ceiling','The smoke','Sweep side to side.'),q('Which is unsafe with home oxygen?','Lighting a candle in the room','Posting an oxygen sign','Securing the tank upright','Keeping tubing out of walkways','Oxygen supports combustion.'),q('Correct body mechanics include:','Bending at the knees and keeping the back straight','Bending at the waist','Twisting while lifting','Holding loads far from the body','Keep a wide base of support.'),q('Soiled laundry should be:','Held away from the body and washed separately','Shaken out first','Mixed with family laundry','Left on the floor','Use gloves.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a home health aide applies infection control and safety practices during a visit to an older client who uses oxygen, checks her blood glucose with lancets and has fallen before.',rubric:[['Infection control',['hand hygiene','gloves','standard precautions','bag','disinfect']],['Sharps and waste',['lancet','puncture','sharps','bag','dispos']],['Oxygen and fire safety',['oxygen','no smoking','flame','candle','race','pass']],['Fall prevention and body mechanics',['rug','clutter','lighting','grab bar','nonskid','body mechanics','ease']]],model:'I would perform hand hygiene on arrival, before and after client contact and before and after gloves, placing my supply bag on a clean surface and keeping clean and dirty items apart. I would wear gloves for any contact with blood or body fluids, disinfect surfaces after glucose checks and bag soiled items before placing them in the trash. Used lancets would go into a labeled puncture-resistant sharps container disposed of according to local rules. Because she uses oxygen, I would check that an “oxygen in use” sign is posted, that no one smokes and that there are no candles or open flames, that tanks are secured upright away from heat and that tubing does not create a tripping hazard; I would know RACE and PASS and check smoke detectors. To prevent another fall, I would remove loose rugs and clutter, ensure good lighting and night-lights, encourage nonskid footwear and the use of grab bars and her walker and have her rise slowly. I would use good body mechanics—wide stance, bent knees, straight back, no twisting—and if she began to fall I would ease her to the floor and report it.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HH,CDC_STEADI,NFPA_O2]};

export const H4={title:'Vital signs: tympanic temperature and apical pulse (Skills I–II)',hook:'Temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure are measured at many home visits, and a change can be the first clue to infection, heart problems or dehydration. Two Round Two skills test vital signs directly, and both award the most points for recording accurately.',objectives:['State normal adult ranges for vital signs and factors that affect them.','Take an adult tympanic temperature following HOSA Skill I.','Take an apical pulse following HOSA Skill II.','Recognize and report abnormal vital signs.'],sections:[
['The vital signs',[
'**Temperature** reflects the balance between heat produced and lost. Normal adult temperature is about **98.6°F (37°C)** orally, with ranges that vary by site: rectal and tympanic readings are typically slightly higher than oral, and axillary readings lower. Fever, infection, exercise and environment raise temperature; older adults may run lower and may not develop a high fever with infection. The **pulse** is the number of heartbeats per minute—about **60–100** in adults; **tachycardia** is above 100 and **bradycardia** below 60. **Respirations** are normally **12–20** per minute in adults, counted without the client knowing. **Blood pressure** below **120/80 mm Hg** is normal for adults; readings of 130/80 or higher are elevated into hypertension categories. Many agencies also measure **oxygen saturation** with a pulse oximeter and ask about **pain** as the “fifth vital sign.” Always follow the ranges and reporting rules in the client’s care plan.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Report or record?',prompt:'Decide which readings are within the usual adult teaching ranges and which you would report to your supervisor.',person:'client',reportTo:'supervisor'}]],
['Taking an adult tympanic temperature (Skill I)',[
'A **tympanic thermometer** measures infrared heat from the eardrum in seconds. It should not be used in an ear with drainage, infection or recent surgery, and earwax can lower readings. In Skill I, the competitor **gathers** the tympanic thermometer, a **disposable probe cover**, the daily record sheet and gloves; performs **hand hygiene and puts on gloves**; and **explains** the skill. They **place a new probe cover**, **turn the thermometer on**, **pull the top of the ear up and back** (the adult technique that straightens the ear canal), **press the top button as they insert the covered probe** into the ear, **hold it in place until it beeps**, remove it and **read** the temperature. They **remove and dispose of the probe cover**, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene, and **accurately record** the temperature on the daily record sheet (4 points), using appropriate communication and standard precautions throughout (24 points; 70% is 16.8).',
order('Tympanic temperature (HOSA Skill I)',['Gather thermometer, probe cover, record sheet and gloves','Hand hygiene and gloves; explain the skill','Place a new probe cover and turn the thermometer on','Pull the top of the ear up and back','Press the button as you insert the covered probe','Hold until it beeps, remove and read','Discard the probe cover, remove gloves and perform hand hygiene','Record the temperature accurately'])]],
['Taking an apical pulse (Skill II)',[
'The **apical pulse** is heard with a stethoscope over the **apex of the heart**, at about the **fifth intercostal space on the left side, in line with the middle of the collarbone** (below the left nipple in many adults). It is used for clients with irregular rhythms, heart disease or certain heart medications. In Skill II, the competitor gathers a **stethoscope, a wristwatch with a second hand, alcohol wipes, a pen and the daily record sheet**; performs hand hygiene; explains the skill; **cleans the stethoscope diaphragm and earpieces** with alcohol; places the **earpieces in the ears** (angled forward toward the ear canals); places the **diaphragm over the apex** of the heart; and **counts the louder sound (“lub”) for one complete minute**. Each “lub-dub” is one beat. They remove the stethoscope, perform hand hygiene and **record the apical pulse within ±2 of the judge’s count** (4 points), with communication and standard precautions (21 points; 70% is 14.7).',
label('heart','Where the heartbeat comes from',[['lv','Left ventricle','Its contraction forms the apex beat heard at the apical site.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Pumps blood to the lungs.'],['mitral','Mitral valve','Closure contributes to the first heart sound (“lub”).'],['tricuspid','Tricuspid valve','Closure also contributes to “lub.”'],['aortic','Aortic valve','Closure contributes to the second sound (“dub”).'],['pulmonary','Pulmonary valve','Closure contributes to “dub.”']]),
chk('How long is the apical pulse counted in Skill II?',['One full minute','15 seconds × 4','30 seconds × 2','10 seconds × 6'],'Count the “lub” sounds for one complete minute.')]],
['Respirations, blood pressure and pulse sites',[
'**Radial** pulse (thumb side of the wrist) is the most common site for routine checks; other sites include the **carotid** (neck, used in emergencies), **brachial** (inner elbow, used for blood pressure and infant CPR) and **pedal** (foot). Count respirations immediately after the pulse while still holding the wrist so the client does not change their breathing; note rate, depth and rhythm, and report shortness of breath, noisy breathing or rates outside the range. Blood pressure is measured with a cuff of the **correct size** on a bare upper arm at heart level, with the client seated and feet flat; the **systolic** number is the pressure when the heart contracts and the **diastolic** when it relaxes. Avoid taking blood pressure on an arm with an IV, a dialysis fistula, a cast or the side of a mastectomy. Some agencies allow aides to use digital monitors; follow the care plan.',
match('Match the pulse site to its location or use',[['Radial','Thumb side of the wrist; routine checks'],['Apical','Over the heart apex with a stethoscope'],['Carotid','Side of the neck; emergencies'],['Brachial','Inner elbow; blood pressure'],['Pedal','Top of the foot; circulation in the legs'],['Temporal','In front of the ear']])]],
['Recognizing and reporting changes',[
'Report vital signs outside the ranges in the care plan, and especially changes from the client’s **baseline**—a pulse that is suddenly irregular, a temperature of 100.4°F or higher in many care plans, rapid or labored breathing, very high or low blood pressure, low oxygen saturation, or new dizziness or chest pain. Record exactly what you measured, the time, the site (for example, “T 99.1°F tympanic”) and any symptoms. Never guess or “round” a value you did not measure; if you are unsure, repeat the measurement or ask for help.',
kase('An unexpected reading',{a:{text:'A client taking a heart medication has an apical pulse of 52 that sounds irregular. She says she feels light-headed. What should you do?',choices:[['Have her sit or lie down safely, record the pulse and symptoms and report to the supervisor immediately','b','Correct. A slow, irregular pulse with symptoms needs prompt reporting.',true],['Record it and report at the end of the week','b','Symptoms with an abnormal pulse require immediate reporting.',false]]},b:{text:'Your supervisor asks whether you counted for a full minute. You counted for 15 seconds and multiplied by 4. What should you say and do?',choices:[['Say so honestly and recount for a full minute','c','Correct. Irregular pulses must be counted for one full minute.',true],['Say you counted for a full minute','c','Honest reporting is essential for safety.',false]]},c:{text:'The full-minute count is 50 and irregular; the nurse arranges an urgent visit and contacts the physician.',end:true}})]],
["Avoiding measurement errors",["Inaccurate vital signs can hide a problem or cause unnecessary alarm. For **tympanic** readings, errors come from a probe cover that is not seated, not straightening the canal (pulling the adult ear up and back), earwax, recent time outdoors in cold weather or measuring right after the client was lying on that ear; wait several minutes and use the other ear if needed. For an **apical pulse**, place the earpieces angled forward, warm the diaphragm, place it on bare skin rather than over clothing, find the apex at the fifth intercostal space on the left, count each **lub-dub as one beat** and count for a full minute. For **respirations**, count one rise and fall as one breath. For **blood pressure**, use the correct cuff size—too small reads falsely high, too large falsely low—and have the client sit quietly for a few minutes with feet flat and arm supported at heart level. Never record a value you did not measure; repeat a measurement that seems wrong and report it.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Will this make a reading inaccurate?","buckets":["Likely to cause an error","Correct technique"],"items":[["Placing the stethoscope over a sweater",0],["Counting lub and dub as two beats",0],["Pulling an adult’s ear up and back",1],["Using a cuff that is too small",0],["Counting the apical pulse for one minute",1],["Seating the probe cover fully",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Adult teaching ranges: temperature about 98.6°F, pulse 60–100, respirations 12–20, blood pressure below 120/80; follow the care plan’s reporting rules.','Skill I: new probe cover, turn on, pull adult ear up and back, press while inserting, hold until beep, record accurately.','Skill II: clean earpieces and diaphragm, place over the apex, count “lub” for one full minute, record within ±2.'],terms:[['Apical pulse','Heartbeat counted with a stethoscope over the apex of the heart.'],['Tachycardia','Heart rate above 100 beats per minute.'],['Baseline','A client’s usual values used for comparison.']],mcqs:[q('For an adult tympanic temperature, the ear is pulled:','Up and back','Down and back','Straight down','Forward','This straightens the adult ear canal.'),q('Normal adult pulse range is:','60–100 beats per minute','30–50','100–150','12–20','Respirations are 12–20.'),q('Where is the apical pulse heard?','Over the apex of the heart on the left chest','At the wrist','On the neck','At the top of the foot','About the fifth intercostal space.'),q('In Skill II, which sound is counted?','The louder “lub”','Both “lub” and “dub” as separate beats','Only “dub”','Breath sounds','Each lub-dub is one beat.'),q('In Skill II, the recorded pulse must be within:','±2 of the judge’s count','±10','±20','Exactly the same','Accuracy earns 4 points.'),q('What is placed on the tympanic thermometer before use?','A new disposable probe cover','Alcohol gel','Lubricant','Tape','Discard it after use.'),q('A heart rate below 60 is called:','Bradycardia','Tachycardia','Apnea','Hypertension','Report if new or symptomatic.'),q('Why count respirations while still holding the wrist?','So the client does not change their breathing','To feel the temperature','To check blood pressure','It is not necessary','Awareness alters breathing.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would take and record an adult tympanic temperature and an apical pulse following HOSA Skills I and II, and explain which findings you would report.',rubric:[['Skill I steps',['probe cover','turn','up and back','button','beep','dispose']],['Skill II steps',['clean','diaphragm','earpieces','apex','lub','one full minute','minute']],['Standard precautions and communication',['hand hygiene','gloves','explain','standard precautions']],['Recording and reporting',['record','daily record','±2','report','supervisor','range','baseline']]],model:'For the tympanic temperature, I would gather the thermometer, a probe cover, the daily record and gloves, perform hand hygiene, put on gloves and explain the skill. I would place a new probe cover, turn the thermometer on, pull the top of the adult’s ear up and back, press the button as I insert the covered probe, hold it until it beeps, remove it and read the temperature, then discard the probe cover, remove my gloves, perform hand hygiene and record the result. For the apical pulse, I would gather a stethoscope, a watch with a second hand, alcohol wipes, a pen and the record, perform hand hygiene, explain, clean the diaphragm and earpieces, place the earpieces forward in my ears and the diaphragm over the apex at the fifth intercostal space on the left, and count the louder “lub” for one full minute, then remove the stethoscope, perform hand hygiene and record the count accurately within two beats. I would report to my supervisor any value outside the care plan ranges or a change from baseline—such as a fever, a pulse below 60 or above 100, an irregular rhythm or symptoms like dizziness or chest pain—immediately.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_VITALS,AHA_BP]};

export const H5={title:'Cardiovascular and respiratory care; elasticized stockings (Skill VIII)',hook:'Many home health clients live with heart failure, high blood pressure or COPD. The aide helps them follow diet and activity plans, use oxygen safely, prevent blood clots with compression stockings and recognize when breathing or swelling means it is time to call for help.',objectives:['Describe common cardiovascular and respiratory disorders and the aide’s role.','Recognize warning signs of heart attack, stroke and breathing distress.','Assist clients with oxygen, nebulizers and breathing techniques safely.','Apply elasticized stockings following HOSA Skill VIII.'],sections:[
['Cardiovascular disorders',[
'The cardiovascular system circulates blood through the heart and vessels. **Hypertension** (high blood pressure) often has no symptoms but increases the risk of stroke, heart attack and kidney disease; clients follow low-sodium diets, take medications as prescribed and have blood pressure checked. **Coronary artery disease** narrows the arteries supplying the heart; **angina** is chest pain relieved by rest or nitroglycerin. A **myocardial infarction (heart attack)** causes chest pain or pressure that may spread to the arm, jaw or back, shortness of breath, sweating and nausea—call 911. **Heart failure** (sometimes called congestive heart failure) means the heart cannot pump effectively, causing fatigue, shortness of breath, coughing and **swelling (edema)** of the feet and legs. Aides weigh clients **daily at the same time with the same scale and clothing**, encourage the prescribed low-sodium diet and fluid limits, elevate swollen legs as directed and report weight gain or worsening breathing. **Peripheral vascular disease** reduces circulation to the legs, causing pain, coldness and slow-healing sores.',
sort('Heart failure sign or not?',['Sign of worsening heart failure','Not a typical sign'],[['Sudden weight gain over a few days',0],['Swollen ankles and feet',0],['Needing more pillows to breathe at night',0],['Persistent cough',0],['Improved energy',1],['Weight loss after diuretic dose adjustment',1]])]],
['Respiratory disorders',[
'The respiratory system brings oxygen in and removes carbon dioxide. **Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**—including **emphysema** and **chronic bronchitis**, usually from smoking—causes shortness of breath, coughing and fatigue. A **nebulizer** turns liquid medication into a mist and is commonly ordered for clients with **emphysema** or other COPD; the aide may assist with setup per the care plan and cleans the equipment. **Asthma** causes reversible airway narrowing with wheezing. **Pneumonia** is a lung infection with fever, cough and difficulty breathing. Aides help clients sit upright, use **pursed-lip breathing** (inhale through the nose, exhale slowly through pursed lips), pace activities, drink fluids unless restricted and avoid smoke and irritants. Report breathing distress, blue or gray lips or nail beds, confusion, fever or a change in sputum.',
label('resp','The respiratory system',[['nasal','Nasal cavity','Warms and filters incoming air.'],['trachea','Trachea','Airway to the lungs.'],['bronchi','Bronchi','Narrow in asthma.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Small airways obstructed in COPD.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Air sacs damaged in emphysema.'],['diaphragm','Diaphragm','Main muscle of breathing.']])]],
['Oxygen and breathing equipment',[
'Oxygen is a medication ordered by the provider at a specific **flow rate**; the aide **never changes the rate**. Clients may use a **concentrator** (an electric device that concentrates oxygen from room air), **tanks (cylinders)** or **liquid oxygen**, delivered through a **nasal cannula** or mask. Aides check that tubing is not kinked, that the cannula prongs are in the nostrils and that the skin behind the ears and in the nose is not irritated (padding and water-based lubricant may help—never petroleum jelly). Safety rules: no smoking or open flames, keep equipment away from heat, secure tanks upright and follow the agency’s instructions. Report if a client seems short of breath despite oxygen, or if equipment is not working.',
chk('A client on oxygen says she feels more short of breath and asks you to turn the flow rate up. What should you do?',['Do not change the rate; check the equipment, help her sit upright and report to the supervisor','Turn the flow rate up','Turn the oxygen off','Tell her to wait until tomorrow'],'Flow rate is ordered by the provider.')]],
['Preventing blood clots: elasticized stockings (Skill VIII)',[
'**Elasticized (compression or antiembolism) stockings** apply pressure to the legs to improve venous blood return and help prevent **deep vein thrombosis** and swelling. They are usually applied in the morning **before the client gets out of bed**, when swelling is least, and must fit smoothly without wrinkles, which can cause pressure injuries. In Skill VIII, the competitor gathers **knee-length elasticized stockings**, performs hand hygiene and explains the skill. With the client **lying supine** in bed, they **expose only the leg indicated in the scenario** (4 points—an important privacy step). They **turn the stocking inside out to the heel** by placing a hand inside and grasping the heel, **position the stocking over the foot and heel** with the heel of the stocking in the proper place, **slide the rest over the foot and heel** and **pull it up the calf to the knee**. They **check that it is smooth and even with no wrinkles**, perform hand hygiene and **record completion and the client’s reaction** (4 points), with communication and standard precautions (25 points; 70% is 17.5). Afterward, check toes for color and warmth and report numbness, pain or skin changes.',
order('Apply an elasticized stocking (HOSA Skill VIII)',['Gather knee-length stockings, hand hygiene and explain','With the client supine, expose only the indicated leg','Turn the stocking inside out to the heel','Position the stocking over the foot and heel','Slide the rest over the foot and heel','Pull the stocking up the calf to the knee','Check that it is smooth with no wrinkles','Hand hygiene and record completion and reaction'])]],
['Recognizing emergencies',[
'Aides call **911** for signs of a **heart attack** (chest pain or pressure, pain spreading to the arm, back, neck or jaw, shortness of breath, cold sweat, nausea), **stroke** (**BE FAST**: balance loss, eye or vision changes, face drooping, arm weakness, speech difficulty, time to call 911), **severe breathing distress** or **choking**. They stay with the client, keep them calm and still, loosen tight clothing and follow agency emergency procedures—including CPR if trained and appropriate. A **do-not-resuscitate order** or advance directive in the home guides emergency decisions, so aides know where it is kept. After the emergency, they report to the agency and document what happened.',
kase('Chest pain at home',{a:{text:'Your client with heart disease suddenly says, “My chest feels heavy,” and is sweating. What should you do first?',choices:[['Call 911, keep him calm and still and notify the agency','b','Correct. These are heart attack warning signs.',true],['Give him one of his wife’s heart pills','b','Aides do not give medications not ordered for the client.',false]]},b:{text:'While waiting, he becomes unresponsive and is not breathing normally. He has no DNR order. What next?',choices:[['Begin CPR if trained and follow the dispatcher’s instructions','c','Correct. Without a DNR, start CPR.',true],['Wait for paramedics without doing anything','c','Every minute without CPR reduces survival.',false]]},c:{text:'Paramedics take over care. You document the timeline and report to your supervisor.',end:true}})]],
["Activity, energy conservation and clot prevention",["Clients with heart and lung disease tire easily, so aides help them **conserve energy**: schedule demanding tasks when the client feels strongest, alternate activity with rest, sit for grooming and kitchen tasks, keep frequently used items within reach and use shower chairs and handheld showerheads. Encourage activity and walking as the care plan allows—movement improves strength and circulation—and watch for shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness or a rapid pulse during activity, stopping and reporting if they occur. Clients may wear their oxygen while moving, with tubing managed to prevent tripping.","Inactivity raises the risk of **deep vein thrombosis**. Prevention includes walking, **leg and ankle exercises** in bed as directed, fluids as allowed, avoiding crossing the legs for long periods and properly applied compression stockings. Report signs of a clot—**swelling, warmth, redness or pain in one leg**—immediately, and **never massage** a painful, swollen leg because a clot could break loose. Sudden shortness of breath or chest pain may signal that a clot has traveled to the lungs: call 911.",{"t":"check","q":"A client’s right calf is swollen, warm and painful. What should the aide do?","options":["Avoid massaging the leg and report immediately","Massage the calf to improve circulation","Apply the stocking tighter","Encourage a long walk"],"why":"These may be signs of a blood clot."}]]
],takeaways:['Heart failure: daily weights at the same time, low-sodium diet, fluid limits as ordered; report weight gain, swelling and breathing changes.','COPD and emphysema may be treated with nebulizers; pursed-lip breathing and sitting upright help; never change oxygen flow and keep flames away.','Skill VIII: client supine, expose one leg, turn stocking inside out to the heel, pull up smoothly to the knee with no wrinkles, record.'],terms:[['Edema','Swelling caused by excess fluid in tissues.'],['Nebulizer','Device that turns liquid medication into an inhaled mist.'],['Deep vein thrombosis','A blood clot in a deep vein, usually in the leg.']],mcqs:[q('A nebulizer would most likely be ordered for a client with:','Emphysema','Iron deficiency anemia','Arteriosclerosis','Congestive heart failure','A Balduzzi sample question.'),q('Clients with heart failure should be weighed:','Daily at the same time with the same scale and clothing','Once a month','Only when swollen','After every meal','Consistency shows trends.'),q('In Skill VIII, the client is positioned:','Lying supine in bed','Standing','Sitting in a chair','Prone','Apply before swelling increases.'),q('The stocking is first turned inside out to the:','Heel','Knee','Toes','Waistband','This makes application smooth.'),q('Why must stockings have no wrinkles?','Wrinkles can cause pressure injuries and restrict circulation','Wrinkles look untidy only','Wrinkles make them warmer','It does not matter','Smooth fit is scored.'),q('Which is a warning sign of a heart attack?','Chest pressure spreading to the jaw with sweating','Mild hunger','Improved energy','A sneeze','Call 911.'),q('Who may change an oxygen flow rate?','Only as ordered by the provider; never the aide','The aide whenever the client asks','The family','Any visitor','Oxygen is a medication.'),q('Which breathing technique helps clients with COPD?','Pursed-lip breathing','Holding their breath','Rapid shallow breathing','Lying flat','It keeps airways open.')],frq:{prompt:'Your client has heart failure and COPD and uses home oxygen. Describe your daily observations and care, how you would apply her elasticized stockings following HOSA Skill VIII, and which changes you would report.',rubric:[['Heart failure care',['weigh','daily','sodium','fluid','edema','swelling']],['Respiratory and oxygen care',['upright','pursed-lip','nebulizer','flow rate','no smoking','tubing']],['Skill VIII steps',['supine','expose','inside out','heel','knee','wrinkles','record']],['Reporting',['weight gain','short of breath','chest pain','blue','report','911']]],model:'Each visit I would weigh her at the same time on the same scale in similar clothing, check her ankles and feet for swelling, encourage her low-sodium diet and fluid limits and help her elevate her legs as the care plan directs. For COPD, I would help her sit upright, coach pursed-lip breathing, pace activities, assist with nebulizer setup and cleaning as allowed and check her oxygen tubing and cannula, never changing the flow rate and keeping flames and smoking away. To apply her stocking, I would perform hand hygiene, explain, have her lie supine, expose only the leg in the scenario, turn the stocking inside out to the heel, place it over her foot and heel with the heel correctly positioned, slide it over the foot and pull it up the calf to the knee, check that it is smooth with no wrinkles, perform hand hygiene and record completion and her reaction, then check her toes. I would report weight gain over the care plan limit, increased swelling, worsening shortness of breath, a new cough, blue or gray lips, confusion or skin problems under the stockings, and call 911 for chest pain or severe breathing distress.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_HF,NHLBI_COPD,NFPA_O2]};
